{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junho","familyName":"Myung","name":"Myung, Junho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeon Su","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Yeon Su","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunwoo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sunwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shin","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Shin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alice","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Alice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PapersPlease: A Benchmark for Evaluating Motivational Values of Large Language Models Based on ERG Theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T07:09:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:22:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-20T05:12:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-21T01:15:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating the performance and biases of large language models (LLMs) through role-playing scenarios is becoming increasingly common, as LLMs often exhibit biased behaviors in these contexts. Building on this line of research, we introduce PapersPlease, a benchmark consisting of 3,700 moral dilemmas designed to investigate LLMs' decision-making in prioritizing various levels of human needs. In our setup, LLMs act as immigration inspectors deciding whether to approve or deny entry based on the short narratives of people. These narratives are constructed using the Existence, Relatedness, and Growth (ERG) theory, which categorizes human needs into three hierarchical levels. Our analysis of six LLMs reveals statistically significant patterns in decision-making, suggesting that LLMs encode implicit preferences. Additionally, our evaluation of the impact of incorporating social identities into the narratives shows varying responsiveness based on both motivational needs and identity cues, with some models exhibiting higher denial rates for marginalized identities. All data is publicly available at https://github.com/yeonsuuuu28/papers-please."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to GEM2 Workshop: Generation, Evaluation \u0026amp; Metrics - ACL 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:31:03Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:31:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:41:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21910","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21910","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21910","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ernie","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Ernie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Huber","name":"Huber, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vish","familyName":"Vogeti","name":"Vogeti, Vish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kant","name":"Kant, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangyang","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikas","familyName":"Chandra","name":"Chandra, Vikas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AutoMixer: Checkpoint Artifacts as Automatic Data Mixers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T04:53:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:18:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-08-01T14:57:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-04T00:43:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-08T16:29:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:12:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In language model training, it is desirable to equip models with capabilities from various tasks. However, it is not clear how to directly obtain the right data mixtures for these capabilities as the relationship between data and tasks is difficult to be modeled. In this work, we observe that checkpoint models exhibit emerging capabilities at different points in the training trajectory. Often, the training process saves checkpoints as artifacts that are under-utilized as a source of in-training data signals. We identify these artifact models based on their respective capabilities on the benchmarks and leverage them as data mixers by using their aggregated first-order influence approximation over source data. We demonstrated on eight reasoning benchmarks that the proposed framework shows significant improvements in the pretraining setting, with performance improvements of up to 1.93%. Overall, this shows the potential of checkpoint models to enhance data quality and optimize data mixtures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21910","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:29:51Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:29:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-10T05:16:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22146","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22146","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22146","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirmohammad","familyName":"Izadi","name":"Izadi, Amirmohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Ali","familyName":"Banayeeanzade","name":"Banayeeanzade, Mohammad Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fatemeh","familyName":"Askari","name":"Askari, Fatemeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Rahimiakbar","name":"Rahimiakbar, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Mahdi","familyName":"Vahedi","name":"Vahedi, Mohammad Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hosein","familyName":"Hasani","name":"Hasani, Hosein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdieh Soleymani","familyName":"Baghshah","name":"Baghshah, Mahdieh Soleymani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Structures Helps Visual Reasoning: Addressing the Binding Problem in VLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T11:44:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:34:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-02T14:31:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-03T00:47:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-08T14:34:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-09T01:22:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-10T11:12:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-11T02:28:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite progress in Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs), their capacity for visual reasoning is often limited by the binding problem: the failure to reliably associate perceptual features with their correct visual referents. This limitation underlies persistent errors in tasks such as counting, visual search, scene description, and spatial relationship understanding. A key factor is that current LVLMs process visual features largely in parallel, lacking mechanisms for spatially grounded, serial attention. This paper introduces Visual Input Structure for Enhanced Reasoning (VISER), a simple, effective method that augments visual inputs with low-level spatial structures and pairs them with a textual prompt that encourages sequential, spatially-aware parsing. We empirically demonstrate substantial performance improvements across core visual reasoning tasks, using only a single-query inference. Specifically, VISER improves GPT-4o performance on visual search, counting, and spatial relationship tasks by 25.0%, 26.8%, and 9.5%, respectively, and reduces edit distance error in scene description by 0.32 on 2D datasets. Furthermore, we find that the visual modification is essential for these gains; purely textual strategies, including Chain-of-Thought prompting, are insufficient and can even degrade performance. VISER underscores the importance of visual input design over purely linguistically based reasoning strategies and suggests that visual structuring is a powerful and general approach for enhancing compositional and spatial reasoning in LVLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025 (Thirty-ninth Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22146","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:35:25Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:35:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-12T03:22:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heting","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Heting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhang","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yunhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuwei","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Zuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepOmni: Towards Seamless and Smart Speech Interaction with Adaptive Modality-Specific MoE"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T02:32:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:14:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-09T03:49:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-10T00:17:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-27T08:52:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:30:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Native multimodal large language models (MLLMs) restructure a single large language model (LLM) into a spoken language model (SLM) capable of both speech and text generation. Compared to modular and aligned MLLMs, native MLLMs preserve richer paralinguistic features such as emotion and prosody, and generate speech responses directly within the backbone LLM rather than using a separate speech decoder. This integration also results in lower response latency and smoother interaction. However, native MLLMs suffer from catastrophic forgetting and performance degradation because the available paired speech-text data is insufficient to support the pretraining of MLLMs compared to the vast amount of text data required to pretrain text LLMs. To address this issue, we propose DeepTalk, a framework for adaptive modality expert learning based on a Mixture of Experts (MoE) architecture. DeepTalk first adaptively distinguishes modality experts according to their modality load within the LLM. Each modality expert then undergoes specialized single-modality training, followed by joint multimodal collaborative training. As a result, DeepTalk incurs only a 5.5% performance drop compared to the original LLM, which is significantly lower than the average performance drop of over 20% typically seen in native MLLMs (such as GLM-4-Voice), and is on par with modular MLLMs. Meanwhile, the end-to-end dialogue latency remains within 0.5 seconds, ensuring a seamless and intelligent speech interaction experience. Code and models are released at https://github.com/talkking/DeepTalk."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:28:47Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:28:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:58:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baohao","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Baohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sara","familyName":"Rajaee","name":"Rajaee, Sara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhui","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Herold","name":"Herold, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anders","familyName":"Søgaard","name":"Søgaard, Anders","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maarten","familyName":"de Rijke","name":"de Rijke, Maarten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christof","familyName":"Monz","name":"Monz, Christof","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lost at the Beginning of Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T09:53:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:29:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T13:32:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:50:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-18T13:52:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:31:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) have significantly advanced complex reasoning capabilities, particularly through extended chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning that incorporates mechanisms such as backtracking, self-reflection, and self-correction. Despite these developments, the self-correction abilities of LLMs during long CoT reasoning remain underexplored. And recent findings on overthinking suggest that such models often engage in unnecessarily redundant reasoning. In this work, we empirically show that the first reasoning step exerts a disproportionately large influence on the final prediction. I.e., errors introduced at this stage can substantially degrade subsequent reasoning quality. This phenomenon is consistently observed across various state-of-the-art open- and closed-source reasoning models. Leveraging this insight, we propose an efficient sampling strategy that leverages a reward model to identify and retain high-quality first reasoning steps while discarding suboptimal ones, achieving up to a 70% reduction in inference cost without sacrificing any accuracy. Our work highlights the central role of the first reasoning step in generating a high-quality reasoning trajectory, and thus enabling significantly efficient sampling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"remove the benchmark part. (10 pages, 6 figures, 5 tables)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:33:19Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:33:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:37:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21875","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21875","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21875","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linhao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bokai","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Bokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuhan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aiwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Aiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WildSpeech-Bench: Benchmarking End-to-End SpeechLLMs in the Wild"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T03:18:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:15:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-31T09:23:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-08-01T00:28:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-26T09:14:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:46:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent multi-modal Large Language Models (LLMs) such as GPT-4o have demonstrated strong capabilities of direct speech interaction. However, the lack of specialized and comprehensive benchmarks for end-to-end speech LLM evaluation hinders optimizing the user experience of Audio LLMs in real-world applications. Existing evaluation methods often adapt text-based benchmarks, overlooking speech's unique characteristics and challenges, including prosody, homophones, stuttering, and differing user expectations. Here, we introduce the first comprehensive benchmark designed to systematically evaluate end-to-end speechLLMs in practical speech conversations. We systematically curate real-world chat data relevant to spoken scenarios, introduce diversity in speaker attributes and acoustic conditions, and augment the dataset with speech-specific phenomena. We further design a query-aware evaluation method to use customized evaluation checklists and prompts to enhance the accuracy of automatic evaluation. We conduct comprehensive testing and detailed analysis of various mainstream speech models, revealing significant differences in model performance across different speech scenarios. The use of query-aware evaluation further enables a finer-grained assessment under various speech-specific scenarios. Our benchmark can provide valuable insights for speech model development and evaluation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21875","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:29:02Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:29:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-29T02:54:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22139","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22139","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22139","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaojie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shaojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqin","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Jianqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenbo","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Zhenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Luan","name":"Luan, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Q-Frame: Query-aware Frame Selection and Multi-Resolution Adaptation for Video-LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T11:30:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:33:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-07T07:52:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-08T01:40:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-22T07:42:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-07-23T00:25:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have demonstrated significant success in visual understanding tasks. However, challenges persist in adapting these models for video comprehension due to the large volume of data and temporal complexity. Existing Video-LLMs using uniform frame sampling often struggle to capture the query-related crucial spatiotemporal clues of videos effectively. In this paper, we introduce Q-Frame, a novel approach for adaptive frame selection and multi-resolution scaling tailored to the video's content and the specific query. Q-Frame employs a training-free, plug-and-play strategy generated by a text-image matching network like CLIP, utilizing the Gumbel-Max trick for efficient frame selection. Q-Frame allows Video-LLMs to process more frames without exceeding computational limits, thereby preserving critical temporal and spatial information. We demonstrate Q-Frame's effectiveness through extensive experiments on benchmark datasets, including MLVU, LongVideoBench, and Video-MME, illustrating its superiority over existing methods and its applicability across various video understanding tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22139","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:35:15Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:35:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-23T02:09:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22049","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22049","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22049","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Pengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajay Kumar","familyName":"Jaiswal","name":"Jaiswal, Ajay Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jishan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shizhe","familyName":"Diao","name":"Diao, Shizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GPAS: Accelerating Convergence of LLM Pretraining via Gradient-Preserving Activation Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T09:45:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:29:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-07-03T16:54:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-07-04T00:47:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern Large Language Models, such as the LLaMA, Qwen and DeepSeek series, predominantly adopt the Pre-LayerNorm (Pre-LN) Transformer architecture. While being stable during pretraining and scalable to large model sizes, Pre-LN suffers from an exponential growth in activation variance across layers, causing the shortcut to dominate over sub-layer outputs in the residual connection and limiting the learning capacity of deeper layers. To mitigate this issue, we propose Gradient-Preserving Activation Scaling (GPAS), a simple technique that can be used in combination with existing approaches. GPAS works by scaling down the intermediate activations while keeping their gradients unchanged. This leaves information in the activations intact, and avoids the gradient vanishing problem associated with gradient downscaling. Extensive experiments across various model sizes from 71M to 1B show that GPAS achieves consistent performance gains. Beyond enhancing Pre-LN Transformers, GPAS also shows promise in improving alternative architectures such as Sandwich-LN and DeepNorm, demonstrating its versatility and potential for improving training dynamics in a wide range of settings. Our code is available at https://github.com/dandingsky/GPAS."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22049","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:33:07Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:33:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-07-04T01:49:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22032","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22032","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22032","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jialei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danda Pani","familyName":"Paudel","name":"Paudel, Danda Pani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luc","familyName":"Van Gool","name":"Van Gool, Luc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroshi","familyName":"Murase","name":"Murase, Hiroshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daisuke","familyName":"Deguchi","name":"Deguchi, Daisuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Partial CLIP is Enough: Chimera-Seg for Zero-shot Semantic Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T09:26:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:28:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Zero-shot Semantic Segmentation (ZSS) aims to segment both seen and unseen classes using supervision from only seen classes. Beyond adaptation-based methods, distillation-based approaches transfer vision-language alignment of vision-language model, e.g., CLIP, to segmentation models. However, such knowledge transfer remains challenging due to: (1) the difficulty of aligning vision-based features with the textual space, which requires combining spatial precision with vision-language alignment; and (2) the semantic gap between CLIP's global representations and the local, fine-grained features of segmentation models. To address challenge (1), we propose Chimera-Seg, which integrates a segmentation backbone as the body and a CLIP-based semantic head as the head, like the Chimera in Greek mythology, combining spatial precision with vision-language alignment. Specifically, Chimera-Seg comprises a trainable segmentation model and a CLIP Semantic Head (CSH), which maps dense features into the CLIP-aligned space. The CSH incorporates a frozen subnetwork and fixed projection layers from the CLIP visual encoder, along with lightweight trainable components. The partial module from CLIP visual encoder, paired with the segmentation model, retains segmentation capability while easing the mapping to CLIP's semantic space. To address challenge (2), we propose Selective Global Distillation (SGD), which distills knowledge from dense features exhibiting high similarity to the CLIP CLS token, while gradually reducing the number of features used for alignment as training progresses. Besides, we also use a Semantic Alignment Module (SAM) to further align dense visual features with semantic embeddings extracted from the frozen CLIP text encoder. Experiments on two benchmarks show improvements of 0.9% and 1.2% in hIoU."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22032","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:32:44Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:32:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:32:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ossi","familyName":"Parikka","name":"Parikka, Ossi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roel","familyName":"Pieters","name":"Pieters, Roel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LMPVC and Policy Bank: Adaptive voice control for industrial robots with code generating LLMs and reusable Pythonic policies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T09:14:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:27:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern industry is increasingly moving away from mass manufacturing, towards more specialized and personalized products. As manufacturing tasks become more complex, full automation is not always an option, human involvement may be required. This has increased the need for advanced human robot collaboration (HRC), and with it, improved methods for interaction, such as voice control. Recent advances in natural language processing, driven by artificial intelligence (AI), have the potential to answer this demand. Large language models (LLMs) have rapidly developed very impressive general reasoning capabilities, and many methods of applying this to robotics have been proposed, including through the use of code generation. This paper presents Language Model Program Voice Control (LMPVC), an LLM-based prototype voice control architecture with integrated policy programming and teaching capabilities, built for use with Robot Operating System 2 (ROS2) compatible robots. The architecture builds on prior works using code generation for voice control by implementing an additional programming and teaching system, the Policy Bank. We find this system can compensate for the limitations of the underlying LLM, and allow LMPVC to adapt to different downstream tasks without a slow and costly training process. The architecture and additional results are released on GitHub (https://github.com/ozzyuni/LMPVC)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by the 2025 34th IEEE International Conference on Robot and Human Interactive Communication (RO-MAN). For further information, videos and code, see https://github.com/ozzyuni/LMPVC"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:32:38Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:32:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:32:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22023","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.22023","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.22023","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanglei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hanglei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hankun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hankun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust and Efficient Autoregressive Speech Synthesis with Dynamic Chunk-wise Prediction Policy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T08:45:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:27:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, autoregressive (AR) language models have emerged as a dominant approach in speech synthesis, offering expressive generation and scalable training. However, conventional AR speech synthesis models relying on the next-token prediction paradigm often encounter significant challenges when handling long speech sequences. These models often struggle to construct stable frame-to-frame attention, leading to increased latency and degraded synthesis quality, thereby limiting their feasibility for real-time applications. To address these limitations, we introduce a novel dynamic chunk-wise autoregressive synthesis framework, termed DCAR, designed to enhance both efficiency and intelligibility robustness in AR speech generation. DCAR introduces a chunk-to-frame attention mechanism through training with multi-token prediction, enabling dynamic chunk prediction in variable speech contexts using a lightweight module trained on-policy. DCAR dynamically adjusts the token prediction span, significantly reducing the sequence length dependency while obtaining high synthesis quality. Comprehensive empirical evaluations demonstrate that DCAR substantially outperforms traditional next-token prediction models, achieving up to 72.27% intelligibility improvement and 2.61x inference speedup simultaneously on the test set. Furthermore, we conduct comprehensive analysis to support it as a versatile foundation for next-generation speech synthesis systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 8 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.22023","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:32:31Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:32:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:32:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21977","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21977","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21977","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StableCodec: Taming One-Step Diffusion for Extreme Image Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T07:39:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:24:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion-based image compression has shown remarkable potential for achieving ultra-low bitrate coding (less than 0.05 bits per pixel) with high realism, by leveraging the generative priors of large pre-trained text-to-image diffusion models. However, current approaches require a large number of denoising steps at the decoder to generate realistic results under extreme bitrate constraints, limiting their application in real-time compression scenarios. Additionally, these methods often sacrifice reconstruction fidelity, as diffusion models typically fail to guarantee pixel-level consistency. To address these challenges, we introduce StableCodec, which enables one-step diffusion for high-fidelity and high-realism extreme image compression with improved coding efficiency. To achieve ultra-low bitrates, we first develop an efficient Deep Compression Latent Codec to transmit a noisy latent representation for a single-step denoising process. We then propose a Dual-Branch Coding Structure, consisting of a pair of auxiliary encoder and decoder, to enhance reconstruction fidelity. Furthermore, we adopt end-to-end optimization with joint bitrate and pixel-level constraints. Extensive experiments on the CLIC 2020, DIV2K, and Kodak dataset demonstrate that StableCodec outperforms existing methods in terms of FID, KID and DISTS by a significant margin, even at bitrates as low as 0.005 bits per pixel, while maintaining strong fidelity. Additionally, StableCodec achieves inference speeds comparable to mainstream transform coding schemes. All source code are available at https://github.com/LuizScarlet/StableCodec."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21977","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:31:26Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:31:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:31:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21972","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21972","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21972","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Ahmed","name":"Ahmed, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Abdelmouty","name":"Abdelmouty, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gunvanth","familyName":"Kandula","name":"Kandula, Gunvanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James C.","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, James C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Advancing Jailbreak Strategies: A Hybrid Approach to Exploiting LLM Vulnerabilities and Bypassing Modern Defenses"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T07:26:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:23:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The advancement of Pre-Trained Language Models (PTLMs) and Large Language Models (LLMs) has led to their widespread adoption across diverse applications. Despite their success, these models remain vulnerable to attacks that exploit their inherent weaknesses to bypass safety measures. Two primary inference-phase threats are token-level and prompt-level jailbreaks. Token-level attacks embed adversarial sequences that transfer well to black-box models like GPT but leave detectable patterns and rely on gradient-based token optimization, whereas prompt-level attacks use semantically structured inputs to elicit harmful responses yet depend on iterative feedback that can be unreliable. To address the complementary limitations of these methods, we propose two hybrid approaches that integrate token- and prompt-level techniques to enhance jailbreak effectiveness across diverse PTLMs. GCG + PAIR and the newly explored GCG + WordGame hybrids were evaluated across multiple Vicuna and Llama models. GCG + PAIR consistently raised attack-success rates over its constituent techniques on undefended models; for instance, on Llama-3, its Attack Success Rate (ASR) reached 91.6%, a substantial increase from PAIR's 58.4% baseline. Meanwhile, GCG + WordGame matched the raw performance of WordGame maintaining a high ASR of over 80% even under stricter evaluators like Mistral-Sorry-Bench. Crucially, both hybrids retained transferability and reliably pierced advanced defenses such as Gradient Cuff and JBShield, which fully blocked single-mode attacks. These findings expose previously unreported vulnerabilities in current safety stacks, highlight trade-offs between raw success and defensive robustness, and underscore the need for holistic safeguards against adaptive adversaries."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21972","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:31:19Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:31:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:31:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21964","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21964","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21964","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael A.","familyName":"Riegler","name":"Riegler, Michael A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristoffer Herland","familyName":"Hellton","name":"Hellton, Kristoffer Herland","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vajira","familyName":"Thambawita","name":"Thambawita, Vajira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo L.","familyName":"Hammer","name":"Hammer, Hugo L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Using Large Language Models to Suggest Informative Prior Distributions in Bayesian Statistics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T07:11:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:22:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Selecting prior distributions in Bayesian statistics is challenging, resource-intensive, and subjective. We analyze using large-language models (LLMs) to suggest suitable, knowledge-based informative priors. We developed an extensive prompt asking LLMs not only to suggest priors but also to verify and reflect on their choices.\n We evaluated Claude Opus, Gemini 2.5 Pro, and ChatGPT-4o-mini on two real datasets: heart disease risk and concrete strength. All LLMs correctly identified the direction for all associations (e.g., that heart disease risk is higher for males). The quality of suggested priors was measured by their Kullback-Leibler divergence from the maximum likelihood estimator's distribution.\n The LLMs suggested both moderately and weakly informative priors. The moderate priors were often overconfident, resulting in distributions misaligned with the data. In our experiments, Claude and Gemini provided better priors than ChatGPT. For weakly informative priors, a key performance difference emerged: ChatGPT and Gemini defaulted to an \"unnecessarily vague\" mean of 0, while Claude did not, demonstrating a significant advantage.\n The ability of LLMs to identify correct associations shows their great potential as an efficient, objective method for developing informative priors. However, the primary challenge remains in calibrating the width of these priors to avoid over- and under-confidence."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21964","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:31:07Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:31:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:31:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21892","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21892","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21892","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Goodge","name":"Goodge, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Hooi","name":"Hooi, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wee Siong","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Wee Siong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyi","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Jingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongyi","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xulei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xulei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SODA: Out-of-Distribution Detection in Domain-Shifted Point Clouds via Neighborhood Propagation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T04:05:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:16:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As point cloud data increases in prevalence in a variety of applications, the ability to detect out-of-distribution (OOD) point cloud objects becomes critical for ensuring model safety and reliability. However, this problem remains under-explored in existing research. Inspired by success in the image domain, we propose to exploit advances in 3D vision-language models (3D VLMs) for OOD detection in point cloud objects. However, a major challenge is that point cloud datasets used to pre-train 3D VLMs are drastically smaller in size and object diversity than their image-based counterparts. Critically, they often contain exclusively computer-designed synthetic objects. This leads to a substantial domain shift when the model is transferred to practical tasks involving real objects scanned from the physical environment. In this paper, our empirical experiments show that synthetic-to-real domain shift significantly degrades the alignment of point cloud with their associated text embeddings in the 3D VLM latent space, hindering downstream performance. To address this, we propose a novel methodology called SODA which improves the detection of OOD point clouds through a neighborhood-based score propagation scheme. SODA is inference-based, requires no additional model training, and achieves state-of-the-art performance over existing approaches across datasets and problem settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21892","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:29:26Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:29:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:29:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21881","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21881","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21881","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyuhng Joon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyuhng Joon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Dual-Layered Evaluation of Geopolitical and Cultural Bias in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T03:37:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:15:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed across diverse linguistic and cultural contexts, understanding their behavior in both factual and disputable scenarios is essential, especially when their outputs may shape public opinion or reinforce dominant narratives. In this paper, we define two types of bias in LLMs: model bias (bias stemming from model training) and inference bias (bias induced by the language of the query), through a two-phase evaluation. Phase 1 evaluates LLMs on factual questions where a single verifiable answer exists, assessing whether models maintain consistency across different query languages. Phase 2 expands the scope by probing geopolitically sensitive disputes, where responses may reflect culturally embedded or ideologically aligned perspectives. We construct a manually curated dataset spanning both factual and disputable QA, across four languages and question types. The results show that Phase 1 exhibits query language induced alignment, while Phase 2 reflects an interplay between the model's training context and query language. This paper offers a structured framework for evaluating LLM behavior across neutral and sensitive topics, providing insights for future LLM deployment and culturally aware evaluation practices in multilingual contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper is accepted to ACL Student Research Workshop (SRW) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21881","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:29:11Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:29:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:29:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21876","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21876","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21876","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiyue","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Qiyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Pi","name":"Pi, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junrong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Junrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruolan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ruolan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinqi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xinqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gautham","familyName":"Kishore","name":"Kishore, Gautham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stone","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Stone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiaxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao-Jung","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Chao-Jung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanyang","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Chuanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiannan","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Jiannan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benhao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Benhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeming","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zeming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Danks","name":"Danks, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianmin","familyName":"Shu","name":"Shu, Tianmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqiao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ziqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianhui","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Lianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiting","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do Vision-Language Models Have Internal World Models? Towards an Atomic Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T03:24:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:15:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Internal world models (WMs) enable agents to understand the world's state and predict transitions, serving as the basis for advanced deliberative reasoning. Recent large Vision-Language Models (VLMs), such as OpenAI o3, GPT-4o and Gemini, exhibit potential as general-purpose WMs. While the latest studies have evaluated and shown limitations in specific capabilities such as visual understanding, a systematic evaluation of VLMs' fundamental WM abilities remains absent. Drawing on comparative psychology and cognitive science, we propose a two-stage framework that assesses Perception (visual, spatial, temporal, quantitative, and motion) and Prediction (mechanistic simulation, transitive inference, compositional inference) to provide an atomic evaluation of VLMs as WMs. Guided by this framework, we introduce WM-ABench, a large-scale benchmark comprising 23 fine-grained evaluation dimensions across 6 diverse simulated environments with controlled counterfactual simulations. Through 660 experiments on 15 latest commercial and open-source VLMs, we find that these models exhibit striking limitations in basic world modeling abilities. For instance, almost all models perform at near-random accuracy when distinguishing motion trajectories. Additionally, they lack disentangled understanding -- e.g., some models tend to believe blue objects move faster than green ones. More rich results and analyses reveal significant gaps between VLMs and human-level world modeling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2025 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21876","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:29:04Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:29:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:29:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21874","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21874","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21874","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stanley","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Stanley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ronik","familyName":"Bhaskar","name":"Bhaskar, Ronik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna Yoo Jeong","familyName":"Ha","name":"Ha, Anna Yoo Jeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shawn","familyName":"Shan","name":"Shan, Shawn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Haitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben Y.","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Ben Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Feasibility of Poisoning Text-to-Image AI Models via Adversarial Mislabeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T03:13:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:15:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3719027.3744845","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Today's text-to-image generative models are trained on millions of images sourced from the Internet, each paired with a detailed caption produced by Vision-Language Models (VLMs). This part of the training pipeline is critical for supplying the models with large volumes of high-quality image-caption pairs during training. However, recent work suggests that VLMs are vulnerable to stealthy adversarial attacks, where adversarial perturbations are added to images to mislead the VLMs into producing incorrect captions.\n In this paper, we explore the feasibility of adversarial mislabeling attacks on VLMs as a mechanism to poisoning training pipelines for text-to-image models. Our experiments demonstrate that VLMs are highly vulnerable to adversarial perturbations, allowing attackers to produce benign-looking images that are consistently miscaptioned by the VLM models. This has the effect of injecting strong \"dirty-label\" poison samples into the training pipeline for text-to-image models, successfully altering their behavior with a small number of poisoned samples. We find that while potential defenses can be effective, they can be targeted and circumvented by adaptive attackers. This suggests a cat-and-mouse game that is likely to reduce the quality of training data and increase the cost of text-to-image model development. Finally, we demonstrate the real-world effectiveness of these attacks, achieving high attack success (over 73%) even in black-box scenarios against commercial VLMs (Google Vertex AI and Microsoft Azure)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACM Conference on Computer and Communications Security 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21874","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-06-30T01:29:01Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:29:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:29:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21873","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2506.21873","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2506.21873","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tzu-Chun","familyName":"Chien","name":"Chien, Tzu-Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chieh-Kai","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chieh-Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiang-Feng","familyName":"Tsai","name":"Tsai, Shiang-Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruei-Chi","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Ruei-Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung-Jen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hung-Jen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Grounding-Aware Token Pruning: Recovering from Drastic Performance Drops in Visual Grounding Caused by Pruning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-06-27T03:11:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06-30T00:15:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have demonstrated strong performance in visual grounding, establishing themselves as a general interface for various vision-language applications. This progress has driven the development of token pruning methods to mitigate the high computational costs associated with processing numerous visual tokens. However, we observe that pruning significantly weakens the model's grounding ability, leading to incorrect predictions and drastic performance degradation. In Referring Expression Comprehension (REC), for instance, pruning causes the accuracy of LLaVA on the RefCOCO validation set to drop from 56.14% to 15.34%. Our analysis identifies misaligned position IDs after pruning as the primary cause of this degradation, as both the order and value of these IDs are crucial for maintaining performance in grounding tasks. To address this issue, we propose Grounding-Aware Token Pruning (GAP), a simple yet effective adjustment to position IDs that recovers REC accuracy back to 51.42%, which is 90% of the original performance in the without pruning setting, all while requiring no additional training, memory, or computational overhead. Applied to models such as Shikra, MiniGPTv2, and the LLaVA series, our method consistently improves performance across various token pruning strategies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.21873","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-06-30T01:29:00Z","registered":"2025-06-30T01:29:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-06-30T01:29:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":19,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=%28doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21864%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21873%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21874%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21875%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21876%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21881%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21892%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21910%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21961%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21964%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21972%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.21977%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22023%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22028%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22032%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22049%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22058%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22139%22+OR+doi%3A%2210.48550%2Farxiv.2506.22146%22%29\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1"}}